  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 AM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nCurrent-Friday...Strong surface high pressure over the Appalachians \ncombined with lower pressures to the south across the Gulf/FL\nStraits and southwest Atlc will keep a tight pressure gradient \nover the area. East winds will increase to 15-20 mph each day with\ngusts to 25-35 mph. Occasional gusts to 40 mph possible at times \nat the coast. Winds remain elevated at night over the interior \n10-15 mph and continued breezy/gusty during the night along the \ncoast. \n\nDeep moisture continues ahead of a weakening low pressure over \nthe Gulf combined with a disturbance aloft to produce NMRS-WSPD \n(60-100%) PoPs thru Fri as low-topped showery precip develops \nacross the local coastal waters and at the coast pushing onshore. \nA Flood Watch remains in effect almost areawide thru Fri. Continue\nto keep Lake County out of the Flood watch given less soil \nsaturation and better ability to absorb several inches over a few \ndays. Primary impacts with showers and ISOLD lightning storms are \noccasional lightning strikes, gusty winds locally 30-45 mph, and \nrepeated torrential downpours. Widespread rainfall amounts of 4-6\"\nare forecast with locally 8-12\" closer to the coast. There is a \nsmall chance for highly localized amounts of 12\"+ focused\nprimarily over portions of Volusia and/or Brevard counties. Do \nnot drive through flooded roadways if you encounter flooding! \n\nA Moderate risk (level 3 out of 4) of excessive rainfall exists for \nthe next three days (today-Fri) along portions of the ECFL Atlc \ncoast with Slight risk (Level 2 out of 4) for much of the interior. \n\nHigh temperatures generally in the L-U70s for most of this period \nwith lows continuing well into the 60s each night and conditions\nhumid.\n\nA High Surf Advisory remains in effect through Fri as winds and \nseas build over the local coastal waters. Rough, pounding surf \nwill promote moderate to locally major beach erosion along the \ncoast. In addition, a HIGH rip current threat will exist. Entering\nthe rough and dangerous surf is strongly discouraged!\n\nSat-Tue...Models remain in fairly good agreement of developing low \npressure near the northern Bahamas by early Fri, lifting this \nfeature northward offshore/parallel to the FL east coast on Sat. \nERLY winds will gradually decrease into the weekend with direction\nbecoming north/northeast Fri evening-Sat afternoon, then further \n\"backing\" to the north/northwest Sat evening into early next week.\nThis should pull in some drier air over the area Sun-Tue.\n\nRain chances decrease to scattered (25-50%) on Sat, with drier \nconditions yet on Sun into early next week. Max temperatures will \nmostly range from the M70s to L80s (south) during this time, with \nmins generally in the L-M60s, except U50s possible for areas \nnorth/west of I-4.",
